HYPOTHESIS (data-grounded, NLSY79 1989, n=12686): Across the 24-32 age window, each additional year of age is associated with a lower probability of dissaving. A logistic regression of DISSAVED on AGE adjusted for GENDER, log(1+INCOME), BMI, BLACK, and HISPANIC with HC1 robust SE yields an AGE coefficient of -0.0467 (SE 0.0092, p=3.51e-07), an odds ratio of 0.954 per +1 year of age (95% CI 0.937-0.972), and an average marginal effect of -0.84 percentage points per +1 year of age. A likelihood-ratio test of an added quadratic AGE term is non-significant (chi-square=4.010, df=1, p=0.045), so the relationship is well described as monotonically decreasing and approximately linear. An LPM with HC1 SE gives an analogous AGE coefficient of -0.0084 (SE 0.0016, p=3.14e-07). On the 8430 respondents whose INCOME/BMI are not mean-imputed, the age effect remains negative and significant: logit coef 0.0001 (SE 0.0107, OR=1.000, AME=0.00 pp/year). An auxiliary multinomial logit over {save-more, same/no-savings, save-less (=DISSAVED)} indicates that per +1 year of AGE the probability of save-less falls by 0.88 pp, the probability of same/no-savings falls by 1.59 pp, and save-more rises by 2.47 pp. Caveats: the empirical age range is only 24-32 (so results describe this 9-year window of young adults, not the full life cycle); about 20-31% of rows show mean-pinned INCOME/BMI values characteristic of mean imputation; survey weights are not available so estimates are unweighted with model-based robust SE; and no marital/employment/education/children controls are in the supplied file. INCOME has no documented scale and is therefore not interpreted in dollars.